      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hickory was laid out in 1856 along the Illinois Central Gulf railroad and named for the large hickory trees dotting the landscape.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hickory%2C_Kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Local landowner Joe Kimble established a store on his property, which served as the nucleus for the community's early settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hickory_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The community was originally known as Hickory Grove before officially adopting the shorter name of Hickory in 1914.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hickory_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Hickory Baptist Church was organized in October 1878 with eleven charter members, providing a focal point for community life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hickory_kentucky"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Hickory remains a quiet, unincorporated locality in Graves County featuring a historic post office as one of its key landmarks.",
